<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Wen of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1050 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Wen of Zhou was the leader of the Zhou people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley in the late Shang period. He built up Zhou power and alliances, and his son King Wu went on to overthrow the Shang around 1046 BCE. Later tradition revered him as a model ruler, and several Zhou states, among them Eastern and Western Guo, were founded by his brothers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou.jpg" alt="King Wen of Zhou" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Wen_of_Zhou_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ji Chang, later called King Wen, was the son of Tairen and of Ji Li, the Elder of Zhou, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> clan of the Shang kingdom along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. In the late 12th century BCE the Shang king Wen Ding is said to have betrayed and executed Ji Li, leaving the young Chang as Elder of the Zhou lineage. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> calls him one of the western barbarians, a phrase commentators read as describing him as close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Xirong</a> and other non-Huaxia tribes.</p><p>According to the Annals of Zhou in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>, he ruled with benevolence, respected the old and welcomed counsellors, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-ziya">Jiang Ziya</a>, and mediated quarrels among the regional lords.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Shang king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/di-xin">Di Xin</a>, fearing Wen's growing power, imprisoned him at Youli after the Marquis of Chong slandered him. His eldest son Bo Yikao was executed when he went to plead for his release. Officials such as San Yisheng and Hong Yao gave Di Xin many gifts, and he freed Wen and invested him as Overlord of the West.</p><p>Wen then began, in the traditional account, to plot against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/di-xin">Di Xin</a>. He settled a land dispute between the states of Yu and Rui, hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-ziya">Jiang Ziya</a> as military counsellor, repelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> and campaigned in turn against Mixu, Li, E and Chong. By then he held about two thirds of the whole kingdom as possessions or sworn allies, and he moved his capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-qi">Mount Qi</a> to Feng.</p><p>He is credited with arranging the eight trigrams into the sixty-four hexagrams of the I Ching and with writing the judgments appended to each. He married Taisi, who bore him ten sons and a daughter, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">Ji Fa</a>, later King Wu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wen died in 1050 BCE before he could cross the Ford of Meng, although other sources suggest he died in battle against the Shang. Tradition placed his burial at Cheng, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>. Four years later his second son Fa crushed the Shang at Muye and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, naming his father Wen, now read as the Cultured. Many of the odes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a> praise him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> counted him a great hero. Tradition holds that Heaven gave him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a> because the virtue of the Shang kings had declined; in 1059 BCE a rare clustering of five planets and an apparition of Halley's comet were seen, and one or more may have been read as a sign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King Wen of Zhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rulers of Chu belonged to the Mi clan, which traced its descent from the mythical Zhuanxu and from Yu Xiong, said to have served <b>King Wen of Zhou</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Eastern Guo was established early in the Western Zhou by Guo Shu or Guo Zhong, younger brothers of <b>King Wen</b>, whose descendants held two separate Guo states.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-guo">Eastern Guo</a></li><li><blockquote>Wu, also called Gouwu, claimed descent from Taibo and Zhongyong, uncles of <b>King Wen of Zhou</b>, who were said to have settled among the tattooed peoples of the south.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-wu">State of Wu</a></li><li><blockquote>Yu traced its founding to Zhongyong, brother of Taibo and uncle of <b>King Wen of Zhou</b>; after the conquest his descendants were confirmed at Yu, near modern Pinglu in south-western Shanxi, on the north bank of the Yellow River.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-yu">State of Yu</a></li><li><blockquote>Western Guo was founded by a brother of <b>King Wen of Zhou</b> near Baoji in the Wei River valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-guo">Western Guo</a></li></ul></section>
